## Test Organization Standards

### 1. Test File Structure
```
tests/ or __tests__/
├── unit/                    # Unit tests
│   ├── services/
│   ├── models/
│   └── utils/
├── integration/             # Integration tests
│   ├── api/
│   └── database/
├── e2e/                     # End-to-end tests
├── fixtures/                # Test data
├── helpers/                 # Test utilities
└── mocks/                   # Mock objects/data
```

### 2. Test File Naming
- **Test files**: `[FileName].test.[ext]` or `test_[filename].[ext]`
- **Integration tests**: `[Feature].integration.test.[ext]`
- **E2E tests**: `[Flow].e2e.test.[ext]`
- **Match source structure**: Mirror the structure of your source code

## Test Structure Patterns

### 1. Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) Pattern

```
[Replace with your language/framework]

Example structure:
describe('FeatureName', () => {
  // Setup
  beforeEach(() => {
    // Common setup for all tests
  });

  test('methodName_withValidInput_returnsExpectedResult', () => {
    // Arrange - Set up test data and dependencies
    const input = createTestData();
    const expectedOutput = {...};
    
    // Act - Execute the code under test
    const result = methodName(input);
    
    // Assert - Verify the outcome
    expect(result).toEqual(expectedOutput);
  });
  
  // Cleanup
  afterEach(() => {
    // Cleanup after each test
  });
});
```

### 2. Given-When-Then (BDD Style)

```
[Replace with your language/framework]

Example structure:
describe('Feature: User Registration', () => {
  test('Given valid user data, When registering, Then user is created', () => {
    // Given (Arrange)
    const validUserData = {
      email: 'user@example.com',
      password: 'SecurePass123!'
    };
    
    // When (Act)
    const result = userService.register(validUserData);
    
    // Then (Assert)
    expect(result).toBeDefined();
    expect(result.email).toBe(validUserData.email);
  });
});
```

## Test Naming Conventions

### 1. Descriptive Test Names

**Pattern:** `methodName_scenario_expectedOutcome`

**Examples:**
```
✅ Good:
- calculateTotal_withValidItems_returnsCorrectSum
- getUserById_whenUserExists_returnsUser
- getUserById_whenUserNotFound_throwsNotFoundException
- processPayment_withInsufficientFunds_returnsFalse
- validateEmail_withInvalidFormat_returnsValidationError

❌ Bad:
- test1
- testGetUser
- itWorks
- basicTest
- shouldPass
```

### 2. Behavior-Focused Names (BDD Style)

```
describe('Calculator', () => {
  describe('add', () => {
    it('should return the sum of two positive numbers', () => {...});
    it('should handle negative numbers correctly', () => {...});
    it('should return zero when adding zero', () => {...});
  });
});
```

## Assertion Patterns

### 1. Common Assertions

```
[Replace with your language/framework]

// Equality
expect(actual).toBe(expected);              // Strict equality
expect(actual).toEqual(expected);           // Deep equality

// Truthiness
expect(value).toBeTruthy();
expect(value).toBeFalsy();
expect(value).toBeNull();
expect(value).toBeUndefined();
expect(value).toBeDefined();

// Numbers
expect(number).toBeGreaterThan(5);
expect(number).toBeLessThan(10);
expect(number).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(5);
expect(float).toBeCloseTo(0.3, 2);          // Floating point

// Strings
expect(string).toMatch(/pattern/);
expect(string).toContain('substring');
expect(string).toHaveLength(10);

// Arrays/Collections
expect(array).toHaveLength(3);
expect(array).toContain(element);
expect(array).toContainEqual({id: 1});

// Objects
expect(object).toHaveProperty('key');
expect(object).toHaveProperty('key', value);
expect(object).toMatchObject({subset});

// Exceptions
expect(() => { throwError(); }).toThrow();
expect(() => { throwError(); }).toThrow(ErrorType);
expect(() => { throwError(); }).toThrow('error message');
```

### 2. Assertion Best Practices

**Do:**
- Use specific assertions (not just `toBeTruthy()`)
- Assert one concept per test
- Use meaningful assertion messages
- Assert both positive and negative cases

**Don't:**
- Have tests with no assertions
- Use multiple unrelated assertions in one test
- Ignore assertion return values
- Assert implementation details

## Mocking and Test Doubles

### 1. Types of Test Doubles

**Mock:** Verify interactions (method calls, parameters)
**Stub:** Return predefined values
**Spy:** Record information about calls
**Fake:** Working implementation (simpler than real)
**Dummy:** Passed but never used

### 2. Mocking Patterns

```
[Replace with your language/framework]

// Mock a dependency
const mockService = {
  getData: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(testData),
  saveData: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(true)
};

// Inject mock
const controller = new Controller(mockService);

// Execute
await controller.processData(input);

// Verify interactions
expect(mockService.getData).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
expect(mockService.getData).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expectedParams);
expect(mockService.saveData).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expectedData);
```

### 3. Mocking Best Practices

**Do:**
- Mock external dependencies (APIs, databases, file system)
- Mock at service boundaries
- Verify important interactions
- Use mock builders for complex setups

**Don't:**
- Mock everything (test becomes meaningless)
- Mock what you're testing (test the real implementation)
- Create brittle tests with too many verifications
- Mock standard library functions unless necessary

## Test Data Management

### 1. Test Data Patterns

**Inline Data:**
```javascript
test('example', () => {
  const testData = { id: 1, name: 'Test' };
  // Use testData
});
```

**Fixtures:**
```javascript
// fixtures/users.js
export const validUser = {
  id: 1,
  email: 'user@example.com',
  name: 'John Doe'
};

// In test
import { validUser } from '../fixtures/users';
test('example', () => {
  // Use validUser
});
```

**Factory Pattern:**
```javascript
class UserFactory {
  static create(overrides = {}) {
    return {
      id: Math.random(),
      email: 'user@example.com',
      name: 'John Doe',
      createdAt: new Date(),
      ...overrides
    };
  }
  
  static createMany(count, overrides = {}) {
    return Array.from({ length: count }, () => this.create(overrides));
  }
}

// In test
test('example', () => {
  const user = UserFactory.create({ email: 'test@example.com' });
  const users = UserFactory.createMany(5);
});
```

**Builder Pattern:**
```javascript
class UserBuilder {
  constructor() {
    this.user = {
      id: 1,
      email: 'user@example.com',
      name: 'John Doe',
      active: true
    };
  }
  
  withEmail(email) {
    this.user.email = email;
    return this;
  }
  
  withInactive() {
    this.user.active = false;
    return this;
  }
  
  build() {
    return this.user;
  }
}

// In test
test('example', () => {
  const user = new UserBuilder()
    .withEmail('test@example.com')
    .withInactive()
    .build();
});
```

## Testing Asynchronous Code

### 1. Async/Await Pattern

```javascript
test('async operation', async () => {
  // Arrange
  const mockData = { id: 1 };
  mockService.getData.mockResolvedValue(mockData);
  
  // Act
  const result = await service.fetchData();
  
  // Assert
  expect(result).toEqual(mockData);
});
```

### 2. Promise Pattern

```javascript
test('promise operation', () => {
  return service.fetchData().then(data => {
    expect(data).toBeDefined();
  });
});
```

### 3. Testing Async Errors

```javascript
test('async error handling', async () => {
  // Arrange
  mockService.getData.mockRejectedValue(new Error('API Error'));
  
  // Act & Assert
  await expect(service.fetchData()).rejects.toThrow('API Error');
});
```

## Test Lifecycle and Setup

### 1. Setup and Teardown

```javascript
describe('TestSuite', () => {
  // Runs once before all tests
  beforeAll(() => {
    // Expensive setup (database connection, etc.)
  });
  
  // Runs before each test
  beforeEach(() => {
    // Reset state, create fresh mocks
  });
  
  // Runs after each test
  afterEach(() => {
    // Cleanup, clear mocks
  });
  
  // Runs once after all tests
  afterAll(() => {
    // Final cleanup (close connections, etc.)
  });
  
  test('example 1', () => {});
  test('example 2', () => {});
});
```

### 2. Test Isolation

**Each test should:**
- Be independent of other tests
- Not rely on test execution order
- Clean up after itself
- Not share mutable state

## Testing Different Components

### 1. Testing Pure Functions

```javascript
// Easy to test - no dependencies, no side effects
test('pure function', () => {
  const result = add(2, 3);
  expect(result).toBe(5);
});
```

### 2. Testing Services/Business Logic

```javascript
test('service method', async () => {
  // Arrange
  const mockRepository = {
    findById: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(userData)
  };
  const service = new UserService(mockRepository);
  
  // Act
  const result = await service.getUserById('123');
  
  // Assert
  expect(result).toEqual(userData);
  expect(mockRepository.findById).toHaveBeenCalledWith('123');
});
```

### 3. Testing Controllers/Handlers

```javascript
test('controller endpoint', async () => {
  // Arrange
  const mockService = {
    getUser: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(userData)
  };
  const controller = new UserController(mockService);
  const req = { params: { id: '123' } };
  const res = {
    json: jest.fn(),
    status: jest.fn().mockReturnThis()
  };
  
  // Act
  await controller.getUser(req, res);
  
  // Assert
  expect(res.json).toHaveBeenCalledWith(userData);
  expect(mockService.getUser).toHaveBeenCalledWith('123');
});
```

### 4. Testing Error Scenarios

```javascript
test('handles error gracefully', async () => {
  // Arrange
  mockService.getData.mockRejectedValue(new Error('Database error'));
  
  // Act
  const result = await service.processData();
  
  // Assert
  expect(result.success).toBe(false);
  expect(result.error).toContain('Database error');
});

test('throws appropriate exception', () => {
  // Arrange
  const invalidInput = null;
  
  // Act & Assert
  expect(() => {
    service.process(invalidInput);
  }).toThrow('Input cannot be null');
});
```

## Test Coverage

### 1. Coverage Metrics
- **Line Coverage**: Percentage of code lines executed
- **Branch Coverage**: Percentage of conditional branches tested
- **Function Coverage**: Percentage of functions called
- **Statement Coverage**: Percentage of statements executed

### 2. Coverage Goals
- Aim for 80%+ coverage on business logic
- 100% coverage on critical paths
- Focus on meaningful tests, not just coverage numbers
- Some code doesn't need tests (trivial getters/setters, generated code)

### 3. Coverage Tools
```bash
# Examples for different ecosystems
npm test -- --coverage              # Jest
pytest --cov=app tests/             # pytest
dotnet test --collect:"XPlat Code Coverage"  # .NET
go test -cover ./...                # Go
mvn test jacoco:report              # Java/Maven
```

## Test Organization Best Practices

### 1. Test Categories

```javascript
// Unit tests
describe('UserService', () => {
  // Group related tests
  describe('createUser', () => {
    test('with valid data', () => {});
    test('with duplicate email', () => {});
    test('with invalid email format', () => {});
  });
  
  describe('updateUser', () => {
    test('with valid changes', () => {});
    test('with non-existent user', () => {});
  });
});
```

### 2. Test Tagging/Marking

```javascript
// Mark slow tests
test('expensive operation', () => {}, { timeout: 10000 });

// Skip tests conditionally
test.skip('not yet implemented', () => {});

// Run only specific tests during development
test.only('debugging this test', () => {});

// Conditional tests
(process.env.INTEGRATION_TESTS ? test : test.skip)('integration test', () => {});
```

## Common Testing Anti-Patterns

### ❌ Don't Do This:

1. **Testing Implementation Details**: Testing how something works, not what it does
2. **Fragile Tests**: Break when implementation changes but behavior doesn't
3. **Slow Tests**: Tests that take minutes to run discourage running them
4. **Flaky Tests**: Tests that sometimes pass, sometimes fail
5. **Testing Private Methods**: Test public interface instead
6. **Too Many Mocks**: More mocks than real code
7. **No Assertions**: Tests that don't verify anything
8. **Shared State**: Tests that affect each other
9. **Testing Frameworks**: Don't test library code
10. **Copy-Paste Tests**: Duplication without parametrization

### ✅ Do This Instead:

1. **Test Behavior**: Test what the code does for users
2. **Stable Tests**: Test against public interfaces
3. **Fast Tests**: Keep unit tests under 100ms
4. **Reliable Tests**: Deterministic, no random failures
5. **Test Public API**: If you need to test private, it might need to be public or separate
6. **Strategic Mocking**: Mock I/O and external dependencies
7. **Always Assert**: Every test should verify something
8. **Isolated Tests**: Each test is independent
9. **Test Your Code**: Focus on your business logic
10. **Parameterized Tests**: Test multiple cases with one test

## Test Documentation

### 1. Self-Documenting Tests

```javascript
// Test name explains what it tests
test('calculateDiscount_forPremiumMembers_applies20PercentDiscount', () => {
  // Arrange section is clear
  const premiumMember = createPremiumMember();
  const originalPrice = 100;
  
  // Act is simple
  const discountedPrice = calculateDiscount(originalPrice, premiumMember);
  
  // Assert is specific
  expect(discountedPrice).toBe(80); // 20% off 100 = 80
});
```

### 2. Comments for Complex Logic

```javascript
test('complex business rule', () => {
  // Given: A user with a premium subscription that expires next month,
  // and they have used 8 out of 10 credits
  const user = createUser({
    subscription: 'premium',
    expiresAt: addMonths(new Date(), 1),
    creditsUsed: 8,
    creditsTotal: 10
  });
  
  // When: Checking if they can perform an action that costs 3 credits
  const canPerform = service.canPerformAction(user, { creditCost: 3 });
  
  // Then: They should not be able to (8 + 3 > 10)
  expect(canPerform).toBe(false);
});
```

## Testing Checklist

Before submitting code:

- [ ] All new code has corresponding tests
- [ ] Tests follow naming conventions
- [ ] Tests are independent and isolated
- [ ] Tests are fast (unit tests < 100ms)
- [ ] Mocks are used appropriately
- [ ] Edge cases are tested
- [ ] Error scenarios are tested
- [ ] Tests are readable and maintainable
- [ ] No skipped tests (or documented why)
- [ ] Test coverage meets project standards
- [ ] Tests pass consistently
- [ ] No console.log or debugging statements

---

**Remember**: Good tests serve as documentation, catch bugs early, enable refactoring with confidence, and make development faster in the long run. Write tests you'd want to maintain.
